// QuantOS — Weinstein Stage Journey + Minervini SEPA
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Two written strategies on one chart, because they answer different halves
// of the same question. Weinstein's stages say WHERE IN THE CYCLE a stock is;
// Minervini's SEPA trend template says WHETHER IT IS IN CONDITION for a VCP
// to form. A Stage 2 name failing SEPA and a SEPA-passing name in Stage 4
// are both worth seeing, and neither is visible if you only draw one.
//
// This file mirrors TWO notes:
//   ```quantos-stages``` in
//     obsidian_vault/QuantOS/brain/Stan_Weinstein_Stage_Analysis.md
//     (evaluated in Python by core/vault/stages.py)
//   ```quantos-rules```  in
//     obsidian_vault/QuantOS/brain/Mark_Minervini_VCP_Strategy.md
//     (evaluated in Python by core/vault/auditor.py)
//
// The notes are the source of truth. This file is a hand transcription of
// both, and scripts/crosscheck_stage_pine.py asserts the constants here match
// the constants there. That check exists because this repo has already
// shipped a Pine/Python divergence: darvasBox() reset boxReady before testing
// for a breakout and silently suppressed nearly every real signal for months
// while the Python engine was fine (fixed 2026-07-18, a74521a). Two
// implementations of one idea drift unless something forces them together.
//
// The Minervini note carries its own scar on exactly this point: its
// distance-from-lows threshold read 2.00 until 2026-08-14, demanding a stock
// had already doubled off its 52-week low, which rejected names in precisely
// the condition the rest of the checklist selects for. Every constant below
// is therefore an input() rather than a literal, so the cross-check can read
// it back out of this file and compare it to the note.
//
// WHY EVERYTHING IS PULLED AT DAILY RESOLUTION
// ────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every input below comes through request.security(..., "D", ...) no matter
// what timeframe the chart is on. Two reasons, and they point the same way:
//
//   1. A stage is a slow, structural state. If the label changed when you
//      flipped from the daily to the 4-hour chart, it would be describing
//      your zoom level rather than the stock. Pinning the computation makes
//      the answer a property of the security.
//   2. The Python side holds daily bars. Anything else here guarantees the
//      two disagree, and the cross-check would have to be abandoned.
//
// Weinstein himself works on a WEEKLY chart with a 30-week average. Both
// implementations substitute a 150-day SMA for it (30 weeks x 5 sessions).
// They are close but not identical: a daily 150-SMA reacts to intra-week
// noise a weekly 30-SMA smooths away, so expect this to flip stage slightly
// earlier and slightly more often than Weinstein's own charts would. That
// caveat is documented in the note and is inherited here deliberately, not
// worked around — working around it here alone would break the mirror.
//
// WHAT SEPA HERE IS NOT
// ─────────────────────
// A PASS means "this name is in the right condition for a VCP", never "a VCP
// is present". The actual heart of a VCP — 2 to 6 contractions, each roughly
// half the last — is NOT tested, by either implementation. The rule DSL has
// no pattern matching over swing structure, and neither does this file. What
// is encoded is the trend template plus the volume dry-up, i.e. the context.
//
// NOT A SIGNAL
// ────────────
// This colours a chart. It places no orders, fires no webhook, and asserts
// no edge. Nothing in the QuantOS vault has been backtested, and neither a
// stage label nor a SEPA verdict is evidence of one. The alerts at the bottom
// are notifications that a state changed, nothing more.

//@version=5
indicator("QuantOS — Stage Journey + SEPA", shorttitle="Stage+SEPA", overlay=true, max_labels_count=500)

// ── Inputs · Weinstein stages ────────────────────────────────────────────
// The flat band is the load-bearing number: it is what separates "rising"
// from "flat", and therefore where every stage boundary falls. The default
// is not a guess — it was picked by running the classifier across the whole
// Nifty 500 at nine widths and choosing the least-churning non-degenerate
// one (scripts/calibrate_stage_band.py). Change it and you are choosing a
// different classifier; re-run that script rather than eyeballing a chart.
bandPct    = input.float(1.0, "Flat band % (30-week MA slope tolerance)", minval=0.0, step=0.25, group="Weinstein stages",
                         tooltip="How much the 150-day average must move over 25 sessions to count as rising or falling rather than flat. Calibrated against the Nifty 500 — see scripts/calibrate_stage_band.py.")
slopeLag   = input.int(25, "Slope lookback (sessions)", minval=5, group="Weinstein stages",
                         tooltip="Sessions back the average is compared against to judge its slope. Mirrors sma(150)[25] in the note.")
priorLag   = input.int(100, "Prior-trend lookback (sessions)", minval=30, group="Weinstein stages",
                         tooltip="Separates Stage 1 from Stage 3, which are identical in a snapshot and differ only in what came before. Mirrors sma(150)[100]. The Python side is capped near 105 by its data fetch; TradingView has more history, but raising it here alone breaks the mirror.")
dryUpRatio = input.float(0.40, "Volume dry-up ratio (2·pivot)", minval=0.05, step=0.05, group="Weinstein stages",
                         tooltip="volume_sma(5) / volume_sma(50) below this marks the quiet pivot zone inside Stage 2 — and is also Minervini's sixth rule. One number, shared by both, exactly as the two notes share it.")

// ── Inputs · Minervini SEPA ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// These are PUBLISHED CRITERIA, not calibrated parameters. They are inputs
// only so scripts/crosscheck_stage_pine.py can read them back and assert they
// still match the note. Tuning them is choosing a different template, and the
// note records what happened last time one of them was wrong.
sepaNearHigh  = input.float(0.75, "Within % of 52-week high", minval=0.1, maxval=1.0, step=0.05, group="Minervini SEPA",
                             tooltip="close >= high(252) * 0.75 — within 25% of the 52-week high. Published criterion; do not tune.")
sepaOffLow    = input.float(1.25, "Above 52-week low, multiple", minval=1.0, step=0.05, group="Minervini SEPA",
                             tooltip="close >= low(252) * 1.25 — at least 25% above the 52-week low. Published criterion. This read 2.00 in the note until 2026-08-14 and silently voided the whole template; see the note before changing it.")
sepaMaSlopeLag = input.int(20, "200-day slope lookback (sessions)", minval=5, group="Minervini SEPA",
                             tooltip="sma(200) > sma(200)[20] — the 200-day higher than roughly one month ago.")
sepaRsMin     = input.float(70.0, "Minimum RS rating", minval=0.0, maxval=99.0, step=1.0, group="Minervini SEPA",
                             tooltip="rs_rating >= 70. See the RS rating input below — this cannot be computed from one symbol's bars.")

// The one rule Pine cannot compute for itself. rs_rating is CROSS-SECTIONAL:
// it ranks this stock against a population, and a chart script sees one
// symbol. The Python side has the same problem and solves it by injection —
// the momentum shortlist supplies a percentile of its 52-week-high ranking.
//
// So this is an input you paste, and 0 means "not supplied". When it is not
// supplied the verdict is INSUFFICIENT_DATA, never PASS, exactly as
// core/vault/auditor.py's _verdict_of does with an unevaluated rule. Five of
// six rules holding is not a pass, and displaying it as one would be the
// single most misleading thing this indicator could do.
rsRating = input.float(0.0, "RS rating from QuantOS shortlist (0 = not supplied)", minval=0.0, maxval=99.0, step=1.0, group="Minervini SEPA",
                       tooltip="Paste the rs_rating the momentum shortlist reports for this symbol. Left at 0 the SEPA verdict is INSUFFICIENT_DATA, which is the honest answer, not a failure. Note this is NOT IBD's RS Rating — different measure, different population — so the >= 70 threshold is directional rather than equivalent.")

cmpSymbol = input.symbol("NSE:NIFTY", "Relative-strength comparison index", group="Minervini SEPA",
                         tooltip="Used ONLY for the informational RS-vs-index ratio in the panel. It is not rs_rating and is never fed into the verdict.")

// ── Inputs · display ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
showBg     = input.bool(true,  "Shade background by stage", group="Display")
showMa     = input.bool(true,  "Plot the 30-week average",  group="Display")
showLabels = input.bool(true,  "Label stage transitions",   group="Display")
showTable  = input.bool(true,  "Show the status panel",     group="Display")
showSepa   = input.bool(true,  "Show the SEPA checklist",   group="Display")

c1 = input.color(color.new(#5B8DEF, 88), "Stage 1 — Basing",    group="Colours")
c2 = input.color(color.new(#2E9E5B, 88), "Stage 2 — Advancing", group="Colours")
c3 = input.color(color.new(#D9A441, 88), "Stage 3 — Topping",   group="Colours")
c4 = input.color(color.new(#D6455D, 88), "Stage 4 — Declining", group="Colours")
c0 = input.color(color.new(#808A94, 92), "Unclassified",        group="Colours")

band = bandPct / 100.0

// ── Daily inputs, pulled once ────────────────────────────────────────────
// One request.security call returning a tuple, rather than several: separate
// calls can resolve against different bars during a partial session, which
// would let the slope test and the prior-trend test disagree about what "now"
// means. lookahead_off is not optional — lookahead_on would let today's chart
// see a daily close that had not happened yet.
//
// dBars is the daily bar count, carried out of the security context because
// the chart's own bar_index counts chart bars, not daily ones, and the SEPA
// warm-up test below needs the daily figure.
[dClose, dSma150, dSma150Slope, dSma150Prior, dVol5, dVol50, dSma50, dSma200, dSma200Prior, dHigh252, dLow252, dClose252, dBars] = request.security(
     syminfo.tickerid, "D",
     [close,
      ta.sma(close, 150),
      ta.sma(close, 150)[slopeLag],
      ta.sma(close, 150)[priorLag],
      ta.sma(volume, 5),
      ta.sma(volume, 50),
      ta.sma(close, 50),
      ta.sma(close, 200),
      ta.sma(close, 200)[sepaMaSlopeLag],
      ta.highest(high, 252),
      ta.lowest(low, 252),
      close[252],
      bar_index],
     lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off)

// Separate call, unavoidably: a different symbol cannot ride in the tuple
// above. It feeds the informational ratio only, never the verdict, so a
// partial-session resolution difference here cannot change a rule outcome.
idxClose    = request.security(cmpSymbol, "D", close,      lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off)
idxClose252 = request.security(cmpSymbol, "D", close[252], lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off)

// ── The stage classifier ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Clause order below is the note's clause order, and it is load-bearing:
// the block is first-match-wins because the stages are mutually exclusive.
// Reordering these branches changes answers. Keep them in the note's order
// even where a different order would read more naturally.
//
// Stage 0 means UNCLASSIFIED, not "stage zero". It is what the Python side
// returns as None, and it appears wherever a term is unavailable — the first
// 150 sessions of a listing, or the first 250 before the prior-trend test can
// run. A warm-up failure must never fall through to a later clause: that
// would let a declining stock be reported as advancing, which is the exact
// failure this project keeps designing against.
warmedSlope = not na(dSma150) and not na(dSma150Slope)
warmedPrior = not na(dSma150Prior)
rising      = warmedSlope and dSma150 > dSma150Slope * (1.0 + band)
falling     = warmedSlope and dSma150 < dSma150Slope * (1.0 - band)
dryUp       = not na(dVol5) and not na(dVol50) and dVol50 > 0 and dVol5 / dVol50 < dryUpRatio
priorUp     = warmedPrior and dSma150Prior > 0 and dSma150Slope > dSma150Prior

int    stage = 0
string phase = ""

if not warmedSlope
    stage := 0
else if falling
    // Checked first, and price above the average does not rescue it: a rally
    // into a FALLING 30-week is a Stage 4 bounce, not a Stage 2.
    stage := 4
else if rising
    stage := 2
    // pivot beats pullback, matching the note's line order — a name can be
    // both quiet and below its average, and the note calls that the pivot.
    phase := dryUp ? "pivot" : (dClose < dSma150 ? "pullback" : "")
else if not warmedPrior
    // Flat band reached, but the prior-trend test cannot run. Unclassified,
    // NOT the Stage 1 default — a stock without enough history is unknown,
    // not basing.
    stage := 0
else if priorUp
    stage := 3
else
    stage := 1

// ── The SEPA trend template ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Six rules, transcribed in the note's own order. Each resolves to one of
// three states, not two:
//
//     1  holds
//     0  fails
//    -1  could not be evaluated
//
// The third state is the whole point. core/vault/facts.py refuses a partial
// window — a "52-week high" computed from 30 bars is not a 52-week high, and
// reporting one would understate the level every comparison is made against.
// Pine's ta.highest() has no such scruple and will happily average whatever
// it has, so the warm-up is enforced explicitly here rather than trusted to
// the built-ins. Without that, this file would report PASS on a six-month-old
// listing where the Python reports INSUFFICIENT_DATA.
SEPA_WARMUP = 252
sepaWarm = not na(dBars) and dBars >= SEPA_WARMUP

ruleCode(bool ok, bool evaluable) => not evaluable ? -1 : (ok ? 1 : 0)

// 1 · Trend stage — the Stage 2 stack, read as one chained comparison
ev1 = sepaWarm and not na(dSma50) and not na(dSma150) and not na(dSma200)
r1  = ruleCode(dClose > dSma50 and dSma50 > dSma150 and dSma150 > dSma200, ev1)

// 2 · MA trajectory — the 200-day higher than ~1 month (20 sessions) ago
ev2 = sepaWarm and not na(dSma200) and not na(dSma200Prior)
r2  = ruleCode(dSma200 > dSma200Prior, ev2)

// 3 · Proximity to highs — within 25% of the 52-week high
ev3 = sepaWarm and not na(dHigh252) and dHigh252 > 0
r3  = ruleCode(dClose >= dHigh252 * sepaNearHigh, ev3)

// 4 · Distance from lows — at least 25% above the 52-week low
ev4 = sepaWarm and not na(dLow252) and dLow252 > 0
r4  = ruleCode(dClose >= dLow252 * sepaOffLow, ev4)

// 5 · Relative strength — injected, never computed. See the input above.
ev5 = rsRating > 0.0
r5  = ruleCode(rsRating >= sepaRsMin, ev5)

// 6 · Volume dry-up — the same expression the stage classifier calls the
//     pivot. Shared deliberately: the two notes write the identical rule,
//     so two copies of it here could disagree.
ev6 = not na(dVol5) and not na(dVol50) and dVol50 > 0
r6  = ruleCode(dryUp, ev6)

// Reduce to one verdict, mirroring core/vault/auditor.py's _verdict_of.
// The ORDER matters and is easy to get backwards: unevaluated rules are
// checked BEFORE failures. A note with one unsupplied fact and one genuine
// failure reports INSUFFICIENT_DATA, not FAIL — because the audit did not
// actually happen, and calling that a rejection would be a claim the data
// does not support.
nUneval = (r1 == -1 ? 1 : 0) + (r2 == -1 ? 1 : 0) + (r3 == -1 ? 1 : 0) + (r4 == -1 ? 1 : 0) + (r5 == -1 ? 1 : 0) + (r6 == -1 ? 1 : 0)
nFailed = (r1 ==  0 ? 1 : 0) + (r2 ==  0 ? 1 : 0) + (r3 ==  0 ? 1 : 0) + (r4 ==  0 ? 1 : 0) + (r5 ==  0 ? 1 : 0) + (r6 ==  0 ? 1 : 0)
nPassed = 6 - nUneval - nFailed

string sepaVerdict = nUneval > 0 ? "INSUFFICIENT_DATA" : nFailed > 0 ? "FAIL" : "PASS"
sepaPass = sepaVerdict == "PASS"

// Informational only. This is a relative-strength RATIO against the chosen
// index over 252 sessions — 1.20 means the stock outran the index by 20%.
// It is NOT rs_rating, which is a cross-sectional percentile against a
// population, and it is deliberately not wired into r5. Two different
// measures with similar names is exactly how a number ends up trusted for
// something it never measured.
rsRatioOk = not na(dClose252) and not na(idxClose252) and dClose252 > 0 and idxClose252 > 0
rsRatio   = rsRatioOk ? (dClose / dClose252) / (idxClose / idxClose252) : na

// ── Bars in the current stage ────────────────────────────────────────────
var int barsInStage = 0
barsInStage := stage == 0 ? 0 : (stage == stage[1] ? barsInStage + 1 : 1)

changed = stage != stage[1] and not na(stage[1])

// ── Rendering ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
stageColour = stage == 1 ? c1 : stage == 2 ? c2 : stage == 3 ? c3 : stage == 4 ? c4 : c0
bgcolor(showBg ? stageColour : na, title="Stage")

// The average is drawn in its SLOPE state, not the stage colour, so the
// instrument and the verdict it produces stay visually distinguishable.
maColour = rising ? #2E9E5B : falling ? #D6455D : #808A94
plot(showMa ? dSma150 : na, "30-week MA (150d)", color=maColour, linewidth=2)

stageName(int s) =>
    s == 1 ? "Basing" : s == 2 ? "Advancing" : s == 3 ? "Topping" : s == 4 ? "Declining" : "Unclassified"

stageLabel(int s, string p) =>
    s == 0 ? "Stage ?" : "Stage " + str.tostring(s) + (p == "" ? "" : " · " + p)

// A tick, a cross, or a question mark — the third glyph carries the third
// state, so an unevaluated rule never reads as a passing one at a glance.
mark(int c) => c == 1 ? "✓" : c == 0 ? "✗" : "?"
markCol(int c) => c == 1 ? #2E9E5B : c == 0 ? #D6455D : #808A94

if showLabels and changed and barstate.isconfirmed
    label.new(bar_index, high,
         text=stageLabel(stage, phase) + "\n" + stageName(stage),
         style=label.style_label_down,
         color=color.new(stageColour, 20),
         textcolor=chart.fg_color,
         size=size.small)

if showTable and barstate.islast
    // Sized once, on the first bar this runs. showSepa is an input and so is
    // constant for the life of the script — without this the panel renders
    // nine empty bordered cells whenever the checklist is switched off.
    var table panel = table.new(position.top_right, 2, showSepa ? 13 : 4, border_width=1)
    table.cell(panel, 0, 0, "Stage",   text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
    table.cell(panel, 1, 0, stageLabel(stage, phase), text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small,
               bgcolor=color.new(stageColour, 60))
    table.cell(panel, 0, 1, "Meaning", text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
    table.cell(panel, 1, 1, stageName(stage), text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
    table.cell(panel, 0, 2, "Sessions in stage", text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
    table.cell(panel, 1, 2, stage == 0 ? "—" : str.tostring(barsInStage),
               text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
    table.cell(panel, 0, 3, "30-week slope", text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
    table.cell(panel, 1, 3, not warmedSlope ? "—" :
               str.tostring((dSma150 / dSma150Slope - 1.0) * 100.0, "#.##") + "% / " + str.tostring(slopeLag) + "d",
               text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)

    if showSepa
        verdictCol = sepaVerdict == "PASS" ? #2E9E5B : sepaVerdict == "FAIL" ? #D6455D : #808A94
        table.cell(panel, 0, 4, "SEPA", text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small,
                   bgcolor=color.new(chart.fg_color, 92))
        table.cell(panel, 1, 4, str.tostring(nPassed) + "/6 rules", text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small,
                   bgcolor=color.new(chart.fg_color, 92))
        table.cell(panel, 0, 5, "Verdict", text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 1, 5, sepaVerdict, text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small,
                   bgcolor=color.new(verdictCol, 60))

        table.cell(panel, 0, 6, "1 · Trend stack",   text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 1, 6, mark(r1), text_color=markCol(r1), text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 0, 7, "2 · 200d rising",   text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 1, 7, mark(r2), text_color=markCol(r2), text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 0, 8, "3 · Near 52w high", text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 1, 8, mark(r3), text_color=markCol(r3), text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 0, 9, "4 · Off 52w low",   text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 1, 9, mark(r4), text_color=markCol(r4), text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 0, 10, "5 · RS rating",    text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 1, 10, r5 == -1 ? "? not supplied" : mark(r5) + " " + str.tostring(rsRating, "#"),
                   text_color=markCol(r5), text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 0, 11, "6 · Volume dry-up", text_color=chart.fg_color, text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 1, 11, mark(r6), text_color=markCol(r6), text_size=size.small)

        // Labelled as a ratio, never as a rating, and visually separated from
        // the numbered rules above it so it cannot be read as a seventh one.
        table.cell(panel, 0, 12, "RS vs index (info)", text_color=#808A94, text_size=size.small)
        table.cell(panel, 1, 12, na(rsRatio) ? "—" : str.tostring(rsRatio, "#.##") + "×",
                   text_color=#808A94, text_size=size.small)

// ── Alerts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// State changes, not trade instructions. Deliberately no webhook payload and
// no alert() call: this indicator has no counterpart on the QuantOS cloud
// side, and giving it one would make a descriptive label look like an
// executable signal.
alertcondition(stage != stage[1] and stage == 2, "Entered Stage 2", "{{ticker}} entered Stage 2 (Advancing)")
alertcondition(stage != stage[1] and stage == 3, "Entered Stage 3", "{{ticker}} entered Stage 3 (Topping)")
alertcondition(stage != stage[1] and stage == 4, "Entered Stage 4", "{{ticker}} entered Stage 4 (Declining)")
alertcondition(stage != stage[1], "Any stage change", "{{ticker}} changed stage")

// Fires only on a real PASS, which requires rs_rating to have been supplied.
// A chart left at the default never fires this, and that is correct: the
// template was never fully evaluated.
alertcondition(sepaPass and not sepaPass[1], "SEPA now passes", "{{ticker}} now satisfies the SEPA trend template")
alertcondition(not sepaPass and sepaPass[1], "SEPA no longer passes", "{{ticker}} no longer satisfies the SEPA trend template")
alertcondition(sepaPass and not sepaPass[1] and stage == 2, "SEPA passes in Stage 2", "{{ticker}} satisfies SEPA while in Stage 2 (Advancing)")

// ── The journey, as a readable series ────────────────────────────────────
// Data-window only, deliberately. This is an overlay indicator, so plotting
// a 1-4 value onto the price scale would flatten the candles into a line at
// the top of the chart. Data-window display keeps the number readable on
// hover — scrub back through the chart and the stage reads out bar by bar —
// without touching the scale.
//
// For a step-line pane of the whole journey instead, copy this file, set
// `overlay=false` in the indicator() call, and delete the bgcolor/plot of
// the moving average. The background shading above already conveys the same
// path on the price chart itself, which is why that is the default.
plot(stage == 0 ? na : stage, "Stage number", color=color.new(stageColour, 0),
     style=plot.style_stepline, linewidth=2, display=display.data_window)

// Same treatment for the SEPA tally, so hovering any historical bar reads
// back how many of the six held at the time. -1 marks bars where the
// template could not be evaluated at all, keeping "unknown" distinct from
// "zero rules held".
plot(sepaWarm ? nPassed : -1, "SEPA rules passing", color=#808A94,
     style=plot.style_stepline, linewidth=1, display=display.data_window)
